        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Structural silicone glazing (SSG) silicones are specifically engineered to have a high dynamic modulus, exceptional tensile strength, and high tear resistance. They form strong covalent bonds with glass and metal mullions. The highly flexible siloxane (Si-O-Si) polymer backbone allows the cured joint to stretch and recover repeatedly under cyclical wind shear and building movements, distributing physical stress uniformly across the structural frame without bond failure."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Why do silicone sealants have superior UV resistance compared to polyurethane sealants?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Silicone sealants are composed of a silicon-oxygen (Si-O-Si) inorganic polymer backbone. The Si-O chemical bond has a high bond energy of approximately 460 kJ/mol, which is stronger than the energy of terrestrial ultraviolet radiation. Polyurethane sealants feature carbon-carbon (C-C) and carbon-nitrogen (C-N) backbones, which have lower bond energies (~348 kJ/mol) that easily break down under solar UV rays, causing polyurethane to dry out, discolor, chalk, and crack."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What is the role of plasticizers in silicone sealant formulation?",
