      <!-- FAQ SECTION -->
      <section class="section" id="faq" style="border-top: 2px solid var(--border); padding-top: 40px; margin-top: 40px;">
        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
        <div style="display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 20px; margin-top: 20px;">
          
          <div class="box" style="margin: 0; background: #ffffff; border: 1px solid var(--border); padding: 20px; border-radius: var(--radius-md);">
            <h3 style="color: var(--primary); font-size: 1.15rem; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px;">1. What is the primary difference between acetoxy and neutral cure silicone sealants?</h3>
            <p style="margin: 0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 0.95rem;">
              The primary difference lies in their chemical curing byproduct. Acetoxy silicones release acetic acid (which smells strongly like vinegar) during curing. This byproduct is acidic and can corrode sensitive metals like copper, brass, zinc, and mild steel, and attack concrete masonry. Neutral cure silicones release non-corrosive alcohols (alkoxy cure) or oximes (oxime cure) during crosslinking, making them safe for metals, concrete, natural stone, and sensitive plastics.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="box" style="margin: 0; background: #ffffff; border: 1px solid var(--border); padding: 20px; border-radius: var(--radius-md);">
            <h3 style="color: var(--primary); font-size: 1.15rem; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px;">2. Why do acetoxy silicones cure faster than neutral cure silicones?</h3>
            <p style="margin: 0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 0.95rem;">
              Acetoxy silicones crosslink via the fast hydrolysis of acetoxysilane groups. The acetic acid byproduct itself acts as a mild autocatalyst for the condensation reaction. Alkoxy-based neutral cure silicones undergo a slower thermodynamic hydrolysis of their alkoxy groups, requiring specialized organotitanate or tin catalysts to drive the reaction, which results in a longer skin-over time and a slower deep cure rate.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="box" style="margin: 0; background: #ffffff; border: 1px solid var(--border); padding: 20px; border-radius: var(--radius-md);">
            <h3 style="color: var(--primary); font-size: 1.15rem; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px;">3. What makes a silicone sealant food-grade and FDA compliant?</h3>
            <p style="margin: 0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 0.95rem;">
              A food-grade silicone sealant is formulated using high-purity polydimethylsiloxane polymers, inert silica fillers, and non-toxic crosslinkers that comply strictly with FDA regulation 21 CFR 177.2600. It must not contain toxic heavy metals (like organotin catalysts above safety limits), phthalate plasticizers, or migratory organic solvents. Additionally, it must be formulated to resist hot water washdowns, oils, and sanitizing chemicals without leaching compounds into contact food.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="box" style="margin: 0; background: #ffffff; border: 1px solid var(--border); padding: 20px; border-radius: var(--radius-md);">
            <h3 style="color: var(--primary); font-size: 1.15rem; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px;">4. How does structural silicone glazing resist severe wind loads and seismic shifts?</h3>
            <p style="margin: 0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 0.95rem;">
              Structural silicone glazing (SSG) silicones are specifically engineered to have a high dynamic modulus, exceptional tensile strength, and high tear resistance. They form strong covalent bonds with glass and metal mullions. The highly flexible siloxane (Si-O-Si) polymer backbone allows the cured joint to stretch and recover repeatedly under cyclical wind shear and building movements, distributing physical stress uniformly across the structural frame without bond failure.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="box" style="margin: 0; background: #ffffff; border: 1px solid var(--border); padding: 20px; border-radius: var(--radius-md);">
            <h3 style="color: var(--primary); font-size: 1.15rem; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px;">5. Why do silicone sealants have superior UV resistance compared to polyurethane sealants?</h3>
            <p style="margin: 0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 0.95rem;">
              Silicone sealants are composed of a silicon-oxygen (Si-O-Si) inorganic polymer backbone. The Si-O chemical bond has a high bond energy of approximately 460 kJ/mol, which is stronger than the energy of terrestrial ultraviolet radiation. Polyurethane sealants feature carbon-carbon (C-C) and carbon-nitrogen (C-N) backbones, which have lower bond energies (~348 kJ/mol) that easily break down under solar UV rays, causing polyurethane to dry out, discolor, chalk, and crack.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="box" style="margin: 0; background: #ffffff; border: 1px solid var(--border); padding: 20px; border-radius: var(--radius-md);">
            <h3 style="color: var(--primary); font-size: 1.15rem; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px;">6. What is the role of plasticizers in silicone sealant formulation?</h3>
            <p style="margin: 0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 0.95rem;">
              In silicone formulations, plasticizers—typically non-reactive polydimethylsiloxane (PDMS) silicone fluids of low viscosity—are blended to reduce the viscosity of the uncured paste for easy application. Post-cure, they reduce the modulus (hardness) of the elastomer, enhancing its joint movement capability and overall flexibility. Non-silicone organic plasticizers are strictly avoided because they eventually migrate to the surface, causing staining of stone substrates and polymer shrinkage.
            </p>
          </div>

        </div>
      </section>

